## nano, the same on every machine: Homebrew's GNU nano on macOS, the packaged nano on Linux and
## the nano inside Git for Windows. Apple's /usr/bin/nano is Pico, which reads no configuration
## file at all, so it never sees this.
##
## this is the shared base, not the file nano reads. zt writes nano's own rc file (~/.config/nano/nanorc
## on macOS, Linux and WSL, ~/.nanorc on Windows) as a copy of this file followed by the colours of
## the Zacess theme it last set and one include line for the syntax definitions this machine has.
## nano accepts set commands only in its main rc file and never in an included one. an include
## that matches nothing is an error. so the file is generated per machine rather than linked.
##
## every option here exists in nano 6.0 (the oldest nano on a supported machine). nano stops at
## start with an error for an option it does not know, so nothing newer goes in.

## the editor
set linenumbers
set softwrap
set atblanks
set tabsize 4
set tabstospaces
set autoindent
set smarthome
set constantshow
set indicator
set mouse
set zap
set trimblanks
set historylog
set positionlog
set stateflags

## the VS Code keys nano can take. nano's own stay beside them: Ctrl+C still shows the cursor
## position, Ctrl+X still exits, Ctrl+O still writes out and Ctrl+W still searches. Ctrl+T then
## Ctrl+Z suspends nano, since Ctrl+Z alone is undo here
bind ^S savefile main
bind ^Q exit all
bind ^Z undo main
bind ^Y redo main
bind ^F whereis main
